This is problematic. When the user clicks the X button, we cancel and prevent closing. But the dialog's own `OnFormClosing` also calls `RequestCancellation()`. So the cancel works but the dialog doesn't close. That's not great UX.

Better approach: Don't register a FormClosing handler. Let the ProgressDialog handle closing on its own. The user clicking X will trigger the dialog's own cancellation logic. The conversion will be cancelled via the CancellationToken.

But wait - if the user clicks X, the ProgressDialog's `Close()` method is called, which calls `RequestCancellation()` (which calls `cancellationSource?.Cancel()`). But we're not passing a `CancellationTokenSource` to the ProgressDialog - we're just passing `progress` and using our own `cts`. The ProgressDialog's own cancellation token is `default` (empty), so its cancel button won't do anything.

Let me reconsider the design. The simplest approach:
1. Don't register FormClosing handler
2. Pass our cts.Token to the ProgressDialog constructor
3. The ProgressDialog will use this token for its cancel button and QueueClose

