content.text:
Wait, returning a dummy ContextMenuStrip from `CreateTimelineContextMenu` is problematic because `ShowMenu` will try to show it. Let me look at `ShowMenu`:

```csharp
private void ShowMenu(Point point)
{
    this.timelineContextMenu?.Close();
    var menu = this.CreateTimelineContextMenu(point); this.timelineContextMenu = menu;
    menu.Closed += (_, _) => { ... };
    menu.Show(canvas, point);
}
```

If `CreateTimelineContextMenu` returns a dummy menu and also shows the video menu inside, the dummy menu will also be shown. That's not ideal.

A better approach: modify `ShowMenu` to check if it's a video clip and handle it differently, or have `CreateTimelineContextMenu` return null and `ShowMenu` check for null.

Actually, let me take a simpler approach. Instead of modifying `CreateTimelineContextMenu`, let me modify `MouseDownTimeline` to check for video clip right-click before calling `ShowMenu`:

Looking at `MouseDownTimeline`:
```csharp
if (e.Button == MouseButtons.Right)
{
    if (this.TryMergeSegmentBoundary(e.Location)) return;
    ShowMenu(e.Location); return;
}
```

I can add a check before `ShowMenu` to see if we right-clicked on a video clip:

input.newString:
// A right click must not collapse an existing range/multiselection or
// silently retarget it to the segment/empty space under the pointer.
if (e.Button == MouseButtons.Right)
{
    if (this.TryMergeSegmentBoundary(e.Location)) return;
    // Right-click on a video clip → video-specific context menu.
    int row = RowAt(e.Y);
    if (row >= 0 && row < Arrangement.Lanes.Count && e.X >= Header && e.Y >= Ruler)
    {
        var videoClip = Arrangement.Lanes[row].Clips.LastOrDefault(c => ClipRect(row, c).Contains(e.Location));
        if (videoClip is { } vc && vc.IsVideo)
        {
            var lane = Arrangement.Lanes[row];
            ShowVideoContextMenu(e.Location, vc, lane);
            return;
        }
    }
    ShowMenu(e.Location); return;
}

